 # Van Buren Sound
 
-**Curved, solid stainless-steel urethral sound** with a smooth rounded tip that follows the J-shaped curvature of the male urethra from the meatus through the bulb and prostatic urethra into the bladder. Graduated in French sizes (typically 8–40 Fr in 2-Fr increments). The Van Buren is the **classic North American urethral sound** — used historically for blind sequential urethral dilation, and still routine for **intraoperative urethral calibration at urethroplasty**, **anterior-bladder tenting for suprapubic-tube placement**, and **exchange-sound work during urethral instrumentation**.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+The Van Buren is a reusable curved metal urethral sound used for urethral probing, calibration, and dilation. Current products differ in working length, French-size range, tip taper, and bend geometry; “Van Buren” does not specify one universal 170-degree curve or 8–40 Fr set.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
 
-## Design
+## Current Configurations
 
-- **Material** — solid stainless steel; reusable, autoclavable.
-- **Curve** — gentle fixed curve concentrated toward the tip (~ 170°), matching the bulbar / membranous bend; **less acute than the [Haygrove](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/haygrove)** (full semicircle for the suprapubic-to-membranous corridor) and **more curved than the straight [Dittel](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/dittel)** female / distal-male sound.
-- **Tip** — smooth, rounded (olive / bullet) to minimize mucosal trauma.
-- **Shaft** — solid, rigid, uniform diameter along the working length (tapered slightly to the tip).
-- **Handle** — flat broad proximal handle for precise control and tactile feedback through the urethra.
-- **Sizing** — French (1 Fr = 0.33 mm), 8–40 Fr, 2-Fr increments; common therapeutic endpoint ~ **24 Fr** (8 mm).
+- Sklar lists an 11-inch, reusable, nonsterile stainless-steel curved Van Buren sound, with individual sizes including a 38-Fr model.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+- The 2026 Greenwald catalog lists a 9.5-inch Van Buren pattern with a 1.75-inch nominal bend height in even sizes from 10 to 30 Fr.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- Other manufacturers list different sets and tapers. Confirm the actual model, markings, surface condition, and reprocessing instructions before use.
 
-## Reconstructive-Urology Uses
+The curved profile can help follow the male urethral course, but anatomy varies and the instrument remains rigid. Curvature is not a substitute for true-lumen confirmation.
 
-### Intraoperative urethral calibration
+## Uses
 
-- **Anterior urethroplasty** — calibrating the proximal and distal lumen at the anastomosis and confirming a 24–28 Fr final caliber at the end of buccal-graft onlay or anastomotic urethroplasty.
-- **Defining normal-caliber urethra at urethroplasty** — passing sequential sounds from each end of the stricture to find the transition into normal-caliber lumen, marking the resection / spatulation point. The ICS male LUTS-surgery terminology document defines urethral calibration as **measurement with "special urethral sounds"** — the Van Buren is the canonical example.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
-- **Exchange sound** for catheter-over-sound techniques, urethral re-entry after diversion, and assisted Council-tip / coude exchanges in the difficult-catheter patient.
+### Urethral calibration and dilation
 
-### Bladder access and suprapubic-tube placement
+Van Buren sounds may be used to calibrate or sequentially dilate the male urethra. The ICS terminology report defines urethral calibration as measurement with special urethral sounds but does not prescribe a Van Buren model or universal final caliber.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-- **Anterior-bladder tenting for SPT** — a Van Buren is passed per urethra into the bladder and used to **tent the anterior bladder wall against the abdominal wall**, creating a palpable / cystoscopically visualized target for suprapubic cystostomy when a dedicated SPT kit is unavailable. **Contraindicated in pelvic trauma** with suspected urethral disruption.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
+Dilation is a treatment for selected urethral strictures, not an inherent indication for every narrowing or difficult catheter. In the older randomized dilation/urethrotomy trial, durability depended strongly on early recurrence and repeated early treatment had diminishing value; the outcome was not specific to a Van Buren product.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
-### Urethral dilation (historical / still common)
+The 2023 AUA amendment permits dilation or DVIU for selected short bulbar strictures and limits urethral DCB recommendations to selected recurrent bulbar disease. Procedure selection, recurrence counseling, and urethroplasty indications belong on the dedicated [DVIU and Urethral Dilation](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu) page.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
 
-- **Blind sequential dilation** in ascending 2-Fr steps; mechanism = **mucosal rupture at the least-scarred portion of the stricture**, producing transient luminal restoration.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
-- **Durability for primary short bulbar stricture** ~ **50–60%**, falling to **~ 20%** for strictures > 2 cm; AUA treats dilation and DVIU as interchangeable first-line options for short bulbar disease with similar long-term outcomes.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)</sup> See the [DVIU](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/dviu) page for the full endoscopic / dilation algorithm.
+### Operative calibration
 
-## Van Buren vs Other Urethral Sounds and Dilators
+Curved sounds may help identify patent urethral segments or calibrate a reconstruction. A practical use does not establish that every anastomosis should be taken to 24–28 Fr; target caliber depends on anatomy, procedure, and surgeon judgment.
 
-| Feature | **Van Buren** | [**Dittel (straight)**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/dittel) | **Filiform + Followers** | [**Balloon Dilator**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator) |
-|---|---|---|---|---|
-| **Shape** | Gentle J-curve (distal) | Straight | Flexible tip + threaded coaxial followers | Inflatable cylindrical balloon |
-| **Material** | Solid stainless steel | Solid stainless steel | Woven / plastic filiform + metal followers | Polyethylene balloon on catheter |
-| **Guidance** | Blind (tactile) | Blind (tactile) | Blind or over-wire | Endoscopic or fluoroscopic direct vision |
-| **Best use** | Male urethral calibration / dilation, anterior urethroplasty | Female urethra or distal male urethra | Tight / impassable strictures | Controlled radial dilation, drug-coated delivery |
-| **Mechanism** | Axial shearing | Axial shearing | Sequential coaxial dilation | **Radial** force |
-| **False-passage risk** | Higher (rigid, blind) | Higher (rigid, blind) | Lower (flexible filiform negotiates lumen) | **Lowest** (direct vision) |
+### Suprapubic-tube placement technique
 
-## Technique for Urethral Dilation
+Wyner described a technique using a curved Van Buren sound, cystoscope, and minor surgical set to place a suprapubic catheter when specialized equipment is unavailable. The method depends on an **intact urethra** and is contraindicated in pelvic trauma; it is one described technique, not a general instruction to tent the bladder blindly.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
 
-1. **Topical 2% lidocaine jelly** instilled per urethra (or regional / GA for difficult cases) plus generous lubrication.
-2. **Begin with the largest sound that passes without resistance** to establish baseline caliber.
-3. **Advance in 2-Fr increments**, pausing at each size to allow tissue accommodation.
-4. **Passage technique** — hold the handle in the dominant hand, introduce the tip with the shaft near-vertical, then **slowly lower the handle toward the patient's feet** as the curved tip follows the bulbar bend into the prostatic urethra and bladder. Advance by **gravity and gentle pressure only — never force**.
-5. **Endpoint** ~ **24 Fr** or the desired caliber.
-6. **Post-procedure** — short-interval catheter at operator's discretion; periprocedural antibiotic prophylaxis given the bacteremia risk of urethral instrumentation.
+## Safe-Use Principles
 
-## Safety Profile
+1. Confirm the indication, stricture site/length, prior instrumentation or reconstruction, urine/infection context, and any concern for urethral injury.
+2. Select the actual curvature and smallest suitable size rather than relying on the eponym.
+3. Use sterile technique, appropriate analgesia/anesthesia, and generous lubrication.
+4. Advance the curve with controlled rotation and alignment to the known urethral course. Never use force or gravity as proof of correct passage.
+5. Stop with bleeding, severe pain, fixed resistance, unexpected trajectory, or suspected false passage.
+6. Increase size only to the minimum caliber needed for the defined goal; no universal 24-Fr endpoint applies.
+7. After trauma or failed blind passage, switch to cystoscopic or otherwise objectively confirmed wire-guided access rather than repeating rigid instrumentation.
+8. Base catheter duration and antimicrobial decisions on the clinical procedure and current guidance.
 
-Complications of urethral dilation with metal sounds:<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+## Comparison Boundaries
 
-- **False passage / urethral perforation** — the most feared complication; rigid blind sounds carry higher risk than visually guided techniques.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Urethral hemorrhage**.
-- **Bacteremia, UTI, sepsis** — periprocedural antibiotic prophylaxis recommended; dilation should not be performed during active UTI.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
-- **Stricture recurrence** — the mucosal-rupture mechanism with periurethral urinary extravasation promotes additional fibrosis, contributing to the high recurrence rates beyond the short-bulbar best case.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+| Instrument | Distinguishing feature | Boundary |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Van Buren** | Curved rigid sound | Blind passage can create or extend a false passage |
+| [**Dittel**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/dittel) | Current patterns may be straight **or curved** | The eponym alone does not select female versus male anatomy |
+| [**Filiform + followers**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/filiforms-followers) | Narrow flexible leader with threaded followers | Requires objective bladder/true-lumen confirmation |
+| [**S-shaped coaxial dilator**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/s-dilators) | Curved dilator over a confirmed wire | Wire misplacement still permits injury |
+| [**Balloon dilator**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/balloon-dilator) | Radial expansion over access | Device-specific indication/IFU; not inherently risk-free |
 
-## Practice Patterns and Current Positioning
+Simple “higher/lower/lowest” injury rankings were removed because comparative risk depends on visualization, access confirmation, disease, product, and operator technique.
 
-Despite guideline encouragement of visually controlled methods, **blind metal-bougie dilation remains common in practice**. A Turkish urology survey found **47% using blind metal-bougie dilation**, 23% plastic dilators over a guidewire, and 26% disposable catheters; non-metal methods were more common in academic centers (p = 0.04).<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> The **2026 EAU guideline** notes that single-session dilation offers outcomes similar to DVIU with **reduced complications when visually controlled**, and identifies the **drug-coated balloon as a promising second-line endoluminal option** for recurrent bulbar strictures.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
+## Complications and Limitations
 
-## Trend Toward Visually Guided and Less Traumatic Dilation
+- False passage, urethral perforation, bleeding, pain, infection, and recurrence.
+- Rigid curvature can be mismatched to an individual urethra or prior reconstruction.
+- Metal sounds do not define the extent of spongiofibrosis or remove the fibrotic substrate.
+- A reusable instrument requires model-specific inspection, cleaning, sterilization, and retirement criteria; complete current IFUs remain unresolved.
 
-- **Direct-vision balloon dilation** — radial force under endoscopic visualization; minimal false-passage risk.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Drug-coated balloon (Optilume)** — paclitaxel-eluting balloon for recurrent bulbar stricture; ROBUST III 1-yr freedom from reintervention 83.2% vs 21.7% for standard DVIU/dilation.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup> See the [Drug-Coated Balloon Therapy](/docs/surgical-techniques/drug-coated-balloon) and [Optilume DCB urethroplasty](/docs/surgical-techniques/04a-urethral-reconstruction/drug-coated-balloon-pointer) pages.
-- **Intermittent self-dilation** with hydrophilic catheters — reduces stricture recurrence by up to 68% after DVIU.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+## Historical Boundary
 
-## Historical Context — William Holme Van Buren
+The sound is associated with American surgeon William Holme Van Buren (1819–1883). The exact design chronology and relationship between current bend-height/taper variants and the original eponymous pattern were not established here.
 
-**William Holme Van Buren (1819–1883)** was a New York surgeon and early American urologic educator, co-founder of Bellevue Hospital Medical College and an early proponent of standardized French-sized urethral instrumentation in the United States. The sound bearing his name remained the dominant metal urethral sound in American urology through the 20th century and persists on every urologic back table today.
